              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price, 5 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,595</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,199 (pre-order)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">On Amazon</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Has been</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The Unsit is built around width and slowness: a 30-inch deck for people who want to forget
            where their feet are, a 2 mph ceiling because it is not meant for anything faster, and a
            lifetime frame warranty. LifeSpan builds narrower machines that go faster and cost far less,
            and publishes daily-use ratings, which InMovement does not. For most people, a LifeSpan’s
            20-inch belt is already wide enough to stop the edge-of-belt problem that cheap 16-inch pads
            have. The Unsit earns its premium for people who find even that too narrow, often heavier
            or broader walkers who spend most of their day on the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            On service, both are small specialist companies and neither has a spotless record. Our
            <a href="/lifespan-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LifeSpan brand guide</a>
            covers LifeSpan’s, and our
            <a href="/lifespan-tx6-under-desk-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LifeSpan TX6 review</a>
            sets out the figures behind the TX6 card above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Who Should Buy an InMovement Treadmill Desk</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy an InMovement Unsit</strong> if you will walk at 1 to 2 mph for several hours of
            almost every working day, you want the widest deck available, and you have a desk with at
            least 40 inches between its legs, or the budget for one. It also makes sense for a shared
            office that wants one machine sturdy and simple enough for anyone to step onto, which is
            where InMovement is now pitching it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the bundle direct</strong> if you are of average height and want the cheapest
            complete set, after confirming the desk top width. <strong>Buy through iMovR</strong> if
            you are tall or want iMovR’s desks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy a LifeSpan instead</strong> if 20 inches of belt is enough, you want to walk
            faster than 2 mph, or the $1,200 difference matters. <strong>Buy a walking pad</strong> if
            you will walk an hour or less a day: our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under desk treadmills</a>
            guide, the <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks</a>
            roundup and the <a href="/best-quiet-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet walking pads</a>
            roundup cover machines from a few hundred dollars.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is InMovement the same company as iMovR?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. InMovement makes the Unsit treadmill and provides its warranty, servicing and technical support. iMovR is a separate retailer that sells the Unsit alongside its own standing desks. On 5 November 2026 InMovement also sold the Unsit direct from its own store.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does the InMovement Unsit treadmill desk cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 5 November 2026 InMovement listed the Unsit treadmill at $2,595, its Unsit Standing Desk at $825, and the two as a bundle at $3,215 against a $3,420 reference price. iMovR listed the treadmill at $2,595 too. Curbside delivery was free from both. Prices change often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the InMovement Unsit specifications?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                InMovement and iMovR agree on a 30 by 40-inch walking surface, 0.3 to 2.0 mph, a 400 lb maximum user weight, a 5-inch step-up, a 162 lb unit weight and a lifetime frame, two-year parts and one-year labour warranty. The motor is given as 2.75 HP by InMovement and as both 2.75 and 2.0 HP by iMovR.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">InMovement vs LifeSpan: which treadmill desk is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on width and speed. The Unsit has a 30-inch belt, a 2 mph ceiling and a lifetime frame warranty for $2,595. LifeSpan’s TX6-GlowUp has a 20-inch belt, 6 mph, the same 400 lb rating and a seven-hour daily-use figure for $1,399. For most desk walkers the LifeSpan is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will the InMovement Unsit fit under my standing desk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It needs at least 40 inches between the desk legs, by InMovement’s figure, and iMovR adds a desktop of 53 inches or wider. Because it raises you 5 inches, your desk also needs to reach about 5 inches higher than your usual standing height. Measure both before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I return an InMovement Unsit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                InMovement offers a full refund within 30 days of delivery minus return shipping, which it says typically costs $250 to $500. The treadmill must be in new condition in its original packaging on its pallet, or a $400 to $500 repackaging charge applies. Delivery and white-glove fees are not refunded.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying an InMovement Treadmill Desk</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The Unsit is an expensive, heavy machine that is costly to send back, so the mistakes that matter are made before the order.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering the bundle without checking the desk height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">InMovement’s desk tops out at 54 inches and the treadmill adds 5. On the belt that is like a 49-inch standing desk. Measure your elbow height standing, add 5 inches, and compare before you pay.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the 48-inch desktop clears the treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Unsit is 39 inches wide and iMovR specifies a 53-inch or wider desktop. InMovement’s compact desk is 48 inches. Ask InMovement to confirm the leg clearance, or choose the 60-inch top.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Throwing away the pallet and packaging</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Both sellers require the original packaging and pallet for a return, and InMovement charges $400 to $500 to repackage without them, on top of $250 to $500 in freight. Keep everything for 30 days.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it for exercise</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Unsit stops at 2 mph and has no incline. It is built for walking slowly while working. If you want a brisk walk, a jog or a workout, a LifeSpan or a folding treadmill does more for less.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

